roduction  joe is if you aren't familiar the cto  of our studio and he was the creator  of the shiny package  after that i want to talk about an  example a prototype app that i built  and then went through steps that joe  outlines in his performance workflow to  take that application  into production and along the way i'm  going to show you what steps i took to  refactor  and then evaluate the impact of that  application  and finally reconsider the hierarchy of  interactivity for what i've produced  so let's jump right into it this is what  i consider  the most important thing for running  shiny in production  and that is you need to have a sandbox  publishing  environment as part of your development  infrastructure  this thing right here you need it  it should be a place where you can  publish freely into  a server that is ideally configured to  be  an identical twin of your production  server  and this is your testing ground you can  call it a sandbox  staging test server whatever you need  one of these  if you only have one server at your  disposal  in a pinch even something like this can  work  you can have the same piece of content  be published to two different  destinations  on a single our studio connect box  and have one destination be you know  reserved for  production while the other destination  is reserved for  testing staging if you  need any help at all in your journey  to production advocating for the right  analytic infrastructure please reach out  to us  at solutions.ourstudio.com where there  are a bunch of resources for having  these conversations  or community.ourstudio.com in the r  admins channel and that's where a lot of  us on solutions engineering like to hang  out and answer questions coming from the  community  so two great resources there  so that was my my most important thing  joe has a very different  perspective and it's equally valid so  these are two  awesome opinions but joe's shiny in  production  most important thing he highlights  in this excellent talk that he gave  at our studio conf 2019 which is also  available  at resources.studio.com webinars  for you to watch this  talk is excellent and this is your  homework from  this webinar is to go watch joe's talk  if you haven't seen it already because  it is really that impactful  if you are doing anything that involves  shiny and production  so this talk was the keynote where he  introduced  the tools that our shiny engineering  team here at our studio  has been working on to help support  bringing  shiny into production so it starts with  going through shiny tests shiny load  tests profis  plot caching and async and he  highlighted  a couple of these tools throughout his  talk but  the thing that's most important to joe  is  profis for shining in production this is  a general profiler for our code but it's  like super important for when you're  bringing  shiny applications into production  a lot of prototype applications fall  over in production  because they contain these performance  anti-patterns  so uh it's important to  when you're trying to turn a prototype  into  a production application to ask yourself  questions  and suss out whether your application is  suitable for production in its current  state  so two questions you can answer  that are great is is this app  fast enough for production and then  if it's not what is the cause  of that performance bottleneck so  through the course of joe's keynote he  goes through and looks at  an example application called cran  whales that he  created and covered  you know how to make that application  faster  through the application of these two  tools shiny load test  to see if the app is fast enough  and profits to see  what in the r code is making that app  not fast enough so you lays out this  excellent performance workflow  which is the real gem here of this talk  uh you you ask if the app is fast enough  you test to see what's making it slow  and then  three is you optimize you solve those  problems  and the importance of using  profis is you can tell how passionate  joe is about this when he talks about it  and  in the keynote he he says you know you  cannot  guess at what is making your our code  slow  you do have to use this profiler because  your  intuition about what is making that code  slow  it stinks it's garbage it's not good  uh like even folks who are very  competent at building shiny applications  can get  tripped up by you know simply assuming  you know what the problem is  so profits is an amazing tool  to help you get to the optimization  stage and then make  real impactful changes to how your  your code works  so in the spirit of joe's talk and his  crayon wales  application success story i wanted to  build a similar application  for this webinar and i won't repeat the  performance workflow demo  that joe has already done in his  definitely go watch it it's really great  seriously  but i wanted to create this prototype to  start that would  realistically highlight some of those  same  or similar performance anti-patterns so  i have a crayon logs app that's called  the trending r packages app and here's  what it does  as you can see here in the screenshot it  has this table  it's a dt table and you can  see which our packages have been  trending  over the last period of time the top 10  and then click on each of these  applications to view a little bit of  metadata  about what that package is and  what it does so all this data is coming  from  the cran logs api project  uh plus a little bit of arvest  magic scraping metadata about each  package so the the workflow that it goes  through  is that i call out to the cram logs api  slash trending endpoint to get those top  10 trending packages  then i make 10 more calls to that cram  logs api to retrieve the download counts  for each package put that data into dt  here in the application and then i use  arvest magic to scrape out that metadata  about each package when at the point the  