ll  so here's one that says uh what if we  don't have  a subscription to shiny connect  so if you don't have our studio connect  there are good alternatives you can  always stand up and open source shiny  server  as long as you have the the requirements  to do so  and we have plenty of resources on shiny  datastudio.com  about how to get started doing that and  a lot of these tools for putting shiny  into production  with the open source server will still  apply  and some will be more difficult to  implement  but you know either way there there is a  path  to doing shiny  sharing shiny assets um  without our pro products let's see  what is required to be able to share a  shiny app with co-workers who do not  have  our studio so today i talked a lot about  um a product called our studio connect  and  i'm sorry if i didn't put a lot of  context around  that you you should definitely go and  watch  tom mock's webinar from two weeks ago  called  our studio connect in production but  like i said  even if you don't go the route of our  studio connect we have  other tools from our studio like shiny  server open source  or even shiny server pro if you're  interested  in alternatives there  here's one that says could gr  could the workflow just described be  done with a  separate shiny app run on a schedule  rather than the r markdown dock  now um shiny applications  don't natively  work running on a schedule  and you have to interact with them or  have  some sort of programmatic trigger to be  able to  do that and they definitely don't run on  a schedule on our studio  connect so if you are trying to  run an etl process on our studio connect  the choice for that would be our  markdown  do the profiling tools work for  shinyapps.io as well  what are the considerations there yeah  so profis is just a general r  profiling code tool so you can  definitely  use that for our code you can use it for  shiny code and you interact with it on  your local side so you're just  profiling